- Do not refactor, redesign, reorganize, rename, consolidate, or clean up unrelated code.
- Prefer additive changes. Preserve existing behavior, architecture, ownership, interfaces, data, and compatibility.
- Apply SOLID, DRY, SSOT, SRP, and Clean Architecture only to changed code. Never use them to justify refactoring.
- Ask before introducing new abstractions, state owners, schemas, protocols, persistence, or migrations.
- Do not create Git worktrees unless the user explicitly requests them.
- Use the smallest focused verification that fits the change, subject to the rule above. Do not run end-to-end tests unless the user explicitly requests them.
- For a long-running or large verification, use a synthetic profile that reproduces the same behavior in less time. Keep the relevant file, state, and failure conditions faithful to the real case.
- Never use mocks, spies, direct state mutation, or implementation-detail assertions.
- Use contract-faithful fakes only for uncontrollable external boundaries.
- Report which focused verification was completed and any verification that remains.
